---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "regions", label: "Regions" },
  { id: "tall-rail", label: "Tall rail" },
  { id: "panes", label: "Panes inside the scaffold" },
  { id: "scrolling", label: "Scrolling" },
  { id: "direction", label: "Right-to-left" },
  { id: "limits", label: "Limits" },
  { id: "accessibility", label: "Accessibility" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="scaffold" sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          A scaffold places an app's bars, rails and panes, so a page doesn't
          have to write its own shell grid. The components inside keep their
          own look and behavior; the scaffold only decides where each one goes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Put <code>.scaffold</code> on a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> that is a
          child of <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>. Not on <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>
          itself: the browser moves the body's overflow to the window, and the
          shell stops clipping. Its direct children are placed by what
          they are: the top app bar <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> spans the top,
          and the <a href="navigation-rail.html">navigation rail</a>,
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> and a vertical
          <a href="toolbars.html">toolbar</a> share the row below it, from the
          leading edge to the trailing one. From 600px up the scaffold is as
          tall as the window and only <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> scrolls; below
          600px the document scrolls. The scaffold is
          CSS only. The app bar's menu button toggles the rail when its
          <code>aria-controls</code> names the rail's <code>id</code>; see
          <a href="navigation-rail.html#outside-toggle">toggling the rail from
          outside it</a>.
        </p>
        <div data-markdown-ignore>
          <div class="scaffold-demo">
          <div class="scaffold">
            <header>
              <div class="bar">
                <button type="button" aria-label="Main menu" aria-controls="scaffold-demo-rail" aria-expanded="false">
                  <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">menu</span>
                </button>
                <h2>Mail</h2>
              </div>
            </header>
            <nav class="navigation-rail" id="scaffold-demo-rail" aria-label="Scaffold demo navigation">
              <a href="#anatomy" aria-current="page"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">inbox</span>Inbox</a>
              <a href="#regions"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">star</span>Starred</a>
              <a href="#scrolling"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">send</span>Sent</a>
            </nav>
            <div class="scaffold-pane" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="scaffold-demo-inbox">
              <div class="p-4">
                <h3 id="scaffold-demo-inbox">Inbox</h3>
                <p>The pane scrolls on its own. The bar, rail and toolbar stay where they are. On a narrow screen the box scrolls instead, with the bar and navigation bar pinned.</p>
                <p style="block-size: 40rem">Keep scrolling.</p>
                <p>End of the inbox.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="toolbar vertical">
              <button type="button" aria-label="Calendar"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">calendar_month</span></button>
              <button type="button" aria-label="Notes"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">sticky_note_2</span></button>
              <button type="button" aria-label="Tasks"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">task_alt</span></button>
            </div>
            <nav class="navigation-bar" aria-label="Scaffold demo destinations">
              <a href="#anatomy" aria-current="page"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">inbox</span>Inbox</a>
              <a href="#regions"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">star</span>Starred</a>
              <a href="#scrolling"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">send</span>Sent</a>
            </nav>
          </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          The demo sits in a box of fixed height, which scrolls in place of
          the window on narrow screens, and uses <code>.scaffold-pane</code>,
          because this page already has its own <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>. In
          an app, write it like this:
        </p>
<Code code={`
<div class="scaffold">
  <header>
    <a class="visually-hidden-focusable" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <div class="bar">
      <button type="button" aria-label="Main menu" aria-controls="rail" aria-expanded="false">
        <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">menu</span>
      </button>
      <h1>Mail</h1>
    </div>
  </header>
  <nav class="navigation-rail" id="rail" aria-label="Primary">
    <a href="/inbox" aria-current="page"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">inbox</span>Inbox</a>
    <a href="/starred"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">star</span>Starred</a>
  </nav>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">…</main>
  <div class="toolbar vertical">
    <button type="button" aria-label="Calendar"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">calendar_month</span></button>
  </div>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.regions}>
        <p>Each region takes one direct child:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Bar: a <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> top app bar, or <code>.scaffold-bar</code>.</li>
          <li>Rail: a <code>.navigation-rail</code>, or <code>.scaffold-rail</code>.</li>
          <li>Pane: <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, or <code>.scaffold-pane</code>.</li>
          <li>Trailing: a <code>.toolbar.vertical</code> or <code>.toolbar-group.vertical</code>, or <code>.scaffold-trailing</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Use the region classes for custom content that isn't one of those
          components. A region with nothing in it takes no space: without a
          rail the pane meets the leading edge, and without a bar the rail and
          pane start at the top. A second child in the same region would
          overlap the first, so give the ones you aren't showing
          <code>hidden</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rail fills its region from the bar down to the bottom of the
          window and scrolls its own destinations when they don't fit. At
          840px and wider, expanding it widens its column and narrows the
          pane. The vertical toolbar is centered in its region, 24px from the
          pane and from the window edge.
        </p>
        <p>
          From 600px to 839px, an expanded rail opens over the pane with a
          scrim instead, and the pane keeps its width. A <code>.modal</code>
          rail does the same at every width. The open rail covers the app
          bar's menu button, so it closes from the scrim, Escape or the rail's
          own menu button. Tab can still move from the open rail to the pane
          under it, as it can from the compact overlay. The rail's column
          keeps <code>--md-comp-nav-rail-collapsed-width</code>, so set that
          token on the scaffold, not on the rail. To take a rail out of the
          layout, give it the <code>hidden</code> attribute; a rail hidden by
          your own CSS still keeps its column on medium windows. On a medium window the
          demo's rail opens over this whole page, as it would in an app.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<div class="scaffold">
  <div class="scaffold-bar">…</div>
  <div class="scaffold-rail">…</div>
  <div class="scaffold-pane">…</div>
  <div class="scaffold-trailing">…</div>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["tall-rail"]}>
        <p>
          Add <code>.tall-rail</code> to run the rail from the top of the window
          to the bottom. The app bar then sits over the pane only, starting at
          the rail's edge, and the trailing toolbar is centered in the full
          window height. Expanding the rail pushes the bar and the pane
          together.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<div class="scaffold tall-rail">
  <header>…</header>
  <nav class="navigation-rail" aria-label="Primary">…</nav>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">…</main>
  <div class="toolbar vertical">…</div>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.panes}>
        <p>
          Put a <a href="panes.html">panes</a> layout inside
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, or put the panes classes on
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> itself. The panes fill the pane region and
          each one scrolls on its own.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<main id="main" class="panes list-detail" tabindex="-1">
  <div class="list-pane">…</div>
  <div class="detail-pane">…</div>
</main>
`} />
        <p>
          Panes choose their columns from the window width. The pane region is
          narrower than the window by the rail and toolbar, so near 840px a
          list-detail layout can show two columns in a narrow region. To make
          the panes follow the region instead, set
          <code>container-type: inline-size</code> on <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>.
          That also makes <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> the containing block for
          anything fixed inside it, such as a FAB, which then scrolls with the
          pane.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.scrolling}>
        <p>
          From 600px up, the scaffold is as tall as the window and doesn't
          scroll the document. <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> scrolls, and so does
          a rail with more destinations than fit. Give
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> <code>tabindex="-1"</code> and a skip link
          that points at it, so keyboard users can move into the pane and
          scroll it with Page Down, Space and the arrow keys. Put the skip link
          inside the <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>: a direct child of the
          scaffold that isn't a region would land in a free grid cell.
        </p>
        <p>
          From 600px up, a top app bar fills with
          <code>surface-container</code> once the pane has scrolled, in
          browsers that support <code>timeline-scope</code>. Elsewhere it
          stays at rest. When a panes layout scrolls inside
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, the bar stays at rest too, since
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> itself doesn't scroll. Below 600px the bar
          fills from the document's scroll, as it does outside a scaffold.
        </p>
        <p>
          Below 600px the document scrolls instead, so a phone's address bar
          can collapse. The bar region stays pinned at the top, and a
          <code>nav.navigation-bar</code> that is a direct child of the
          scaffold, or the only child of its direct-child
          <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code>, stays
          pinned at the bottom. The navigation bar only shows below 600px. The
          rail is hidden and out of the tab order until the app bar's menu
          button opens it over the page with a scrim, so the app bar needs a
          button whose <code>aria-controls</code> names the rail. Custom
          <code>.scaffold-rail</code> content is hidden too. Don't put
          <code>.hide-on-compact-only</code> on the rail: it would keep the
          rail from ever opening.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the scaffold is a direct child of <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>,
          the document gets scroll padding below 600px, so keyboard focus and
          links to anchors land clear of the two pinned bars. The padding fits
          the small app bar and a navigation bar with one-line labels; set a
          larger <code>scroll-padding-block</code> on <code>:root</code> for
          taller bars.
        </p>
        <p>
          A vertical toolbar in the trailing region turns into a centered row
          below 600px and floats 16px above the navigation bar, or above the
          bottom edge when there is none. Its actions keep their order, and
          left tooltips show above them. Its row stays at the end of the page,
          so the last content scrolls clear of it, and the scroll padding
          grows so focus clears it too. Keep it to a few actions on a phone.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<div class="scaffold">
  <header>…</header>
  <nav class="navigation-rail" id="rail" aria-label="Primary">…</nav>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">…</main>
  <footer>
    <nav class="navigation-bar" aria-label="Primary">
      <a href="/inbox" aria-current="page"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">inbox</span>Inbox</a>
      <a href="/starred"><span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">star</span>Starred</a>
    </nav>
  </footer>
</div>
`} />
        <p>
          Landscape phones wider than 600px get the medium layout, where the
          pane scrolls. A panes layout inside <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> grows
          with its content below 600px, so the page scrolls around it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Printing drops the fixed height, so the whole pane prints. The rail
          and the navigation bar don't print.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.direction}>
        <p>
          The regions follow the writing direction. Under
          <code>dir="rtl"</code> the rail is on the right and the toolbar on
          the left.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.limits}>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <code>.medium</code> and <code>.large</code> app bars stay expanded
            in a scaffold, even below 600px. Use the small bar.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="back-to-top.html">Back to top</a> watches the window, so
            in a scaffold it appears only below 600px, where it overlaps the
            navigation bar as it does any bottom bar.
          </li>
          <li>
            The rail opens and closes without animating on compact windows,
            and a scrim tap leaves focus on the page; Escape returns it to the
            menu button.
          </li>
          <li>
            A navigation rail's side panel spans the window height, so it opens
            over the bar in either layout.
          </li>
          <li>
            A trailing toolbar taller than its region is cut off at the
            bottom, so keep it to the actions that fit. Custom
            <code>.scaffold-rail</code> and <code>.scaffold-trailing</code>
            content scrolls instead.
          </li>
          <li>
            Other children aren't placed. They fill whatever grid cell is free.
          </li>
        </ul>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.accessibility}>
        <p>
          The scaffold adds no landmark. Its <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> is the
          page's banner, the navigation rail is a navigation landmark, and
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> is the main landmark, in that order. Write
          the scaffold as a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, never as a landmark element or with a
          landmark role. A page has one scaffold; use panes or the grid inside
          <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> for more columns.
        </p>
  </Section>
</DocsLayout>
